A||N||15||M King||Reliability/Sportyness Ratio||||5||
Z||000000||M King||12-05-1999||08:56 PM||||Hi All,<BR>I have been reading this this Forum for a few months now. I know you must get this question ALLOT, but I will ask it anyways :-). Well here goes... I am going to be buying a fiero for my first car in a few months, and I really would like to know what you ppl think is the best fiero in sportyness and reliability. I would like a 6cylinder, but this would be used as a daily driver so reliability is probably more important.Price also has to be considered a bit (especially insurance I think a GT must have higher insurance prices the like a coupe or 2m6), but most fieros arnt "that" expensive.Thanks a bunch.. Keep up the GREAT Forum! I have been enjoying it for a while now :-)||||reg||5
Z||000001||batboy||12-05-1999||09:22 PM||||Well, I think most of us will agree that the 1988 Fieros have slightly better brakes and suspension, but they are also usually the most expensive. I'm not sure if there's much difference in insurance rates if any between the GT models and the rest, but if you prefer not getting a GT, then a later model SE with a V6 is a great choice and usually cheaper too. A well maintained V6 is very reliable and fun to drive. Good luck.||||reg||1
Z||000002||william_thorniii||12-05-1999||09:40 PM||||Both in Ohio and Michigan, I know the insurance is the same for everything past a certain year.  ALL Fieros (including 88's) cost the same as my old Dodge PU.  I know that I am going to get flamed for this one... but I do not feel that the advantages of a 1988 Fiero are worth the additional price they often draw.  I am not saying that their suspension/brake upgrades are not better than previous years.... O.K. flame if you feel that you need to, but please be nice.  <P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||1
Z||000003||RZURCTN||12-05-1999||10:16 PM||||We don't flame around here.  You are as entitled to your opinion as the next guy!  Just have a little respect for what others think too.  Fieros are as different as each of our own personalities.  Indeed, I believe that we all put a little of ourselves into our Fiero, it's what makes us all unique.  Yet we all have something in common; our fondness of a certain machine, made by a certain company, and for a limited time.  By the way M KING, I don't think it matters what year/model you buy.  If you take good care of it, it will take good care of you!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  ||||reg||1
Z||000004||RZURCTN||12-05-1999||10:18 PM||||P.S.:  Welcome to the Forum!  Hope you enjoy as much as the rest of us!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||10
Z||000005||SCCA FIERO||12-05-1999||10:43 PM||||I have autocrossed both my 86SE and my 88 coupe with a 4 and a V6. If you were to put each year to the test like that you would think different. I thought the 86 had excessive rearend dancing and tracking. The 88 is more predictable and balanced(espically with the 4 cylinder in it, but no guts). Also the brakes are a night and day improvement. The 88 requires less effort and I didn't notice any fade from my 88 like my 86 was plagued with. Lastly... the 88 suspension just looks better. I had my cradle on the ground, both the 86 and 88, and the 88 was more pleasing to look at. Not flaming... just voicing my thoughts.||||reg||12
Z||000006||batboy||12-06-1999||12:08 AM||||It's probably more of a matter of how you drive your car and what it's used for. If a person is into performance driving or SCCA racing, then by all means, the 88 models have an edge. I think what William is trying to say is, if the car is just for fun and normal everyday street cruising, then it really don't matter. Besides, you can make an earlier Fiero brake and handle as well if not better than the 88's, it just takes a lot more money and work. They're all good.||||reg||1
Z||000007||RZURCTN||12-06-1999||08:14 AM||||Well M KING, I guess that the best thing for you to do would be to shop around a little and find one you like.  Test drive it, ask lots of questions (especially about routine maintenance), and if you're not too familiar with the Fiero, have it looked over by someone who is.  Find a mechanic you can trust and who knows how to work on a Fiero.  Good luck and Happy Hunting!! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000008||Monkeyman||12-06-1999||09:01 AM||||Good luck trying to find a mechanic who's familiar with the Fiero.  Ask one to check the oil and see which "hood" he opens:-)  The '88s do have a better susp and brakes, but most of them that I've seen cost more, too.  There are alot of things you can do to a pre-'88 to improve these areas.  The Grand Am brake upgrade will improve the brakes so that they are at least as good if not better than the '88s and it's cheaper than replacing the entire system with stock parts, too.  As for the susp., you can spend a little or a lot depending on what you want.  I've seen some with $800 worth of new susp stuff (good shocks/struts, lowering springs, etc) that will make your car handle like it's on rails.  Also, if you want a GT and don't mind the notchback style (personally I like it better), try an '86 SE.  It had the GT body, but not some of the emblems, etc.  It can be had cheaper than a "real" GT.  Actually, if you don't mind spending a little bit of time, you can have a GT clone for a lot less than a "real" one by purchasing a base model and adding stuff later.  (I have an '86 2m4, but give me about a year and you won't be able to tell it apart from a GT unless you check the VIN)||||reg||1
Z||000009||ChadMan||12-06-1999||09:34 AM||||Two rules of thumb to follow:<P>1)  Later-model Fieros tend to be more reliable than earlier-model Fieros.<BR>2)  Fiero clutches are weak.  If you buy a stickshift Fiero, sooner or later, you're going to have to put a clutch in it.<P>Other than that, Fieros are every bit as reliable as any other 80's GM car.  My 88 Coupe has been the most reliable car I've ever owned - in the past 30K miles, no expenses but gas, oil changes and lower ball joints.||||reg||1
Z||000010||M King||12-06-1999||04:18 PM||||Thanks for all the great replys!<BR>I think im going to start looking for a fiero in a month or two, I will probably get a 88, but if I can get one for a really good price I really dont mind what year it is. I can allways upgrade it. Once again thanks allot!<P>and im very happy to be part of your fiero group :-)<BR>||||reg||14
Z||000011||Formula||12-06-1999||06:53 PM||||I have an 88 Formula. In about 6 months its went through a; water pump($20), alternator($100), spark plug wires dist. cap and rotor (tune up)and spark plugs($50), vacuum can ($5). It is fairly reliable. The alternator still worked but had a worn bearing and sounded bad, the water pump pulley came off so I replaced the whole thing, It was missing about twice a day so I tuned it up and it stopped. And the egr tube is blocked off now but I'm working on finding a cheap replacement. <BR>FIEROS RULE, EVERYTHING ELSE DROOLS.<P>I don't think if you just drive regularly the 88s differ much from a well mainted pre88<P>I'm 18 and so far the repairs have been straight forward, GOOD LUCK||||reg||1
Z||000012||Shiner||12-07-1999||04:17 AM||||No disrespect to '84 owners (I am one of them!), but driving my '87 GT instead of my driving my 84 SC is like driving a compleatly different car!  Most of the bad things about the way my '84 handles are non-existant in my GT.  It handles WAY better over rough stuff. It doesn't understeer off the side of the road; it's very neutral, with a hint of understeer.  I would recomend getting as late  model of a fiero you can.  I havn't driven an '88, so I don't know how they compare.||||reg||1
Z||000013||fiero56||12-07-1999||08:47 AM||||I don't know know what different years drive like,(I've only driven one Fiero, mine), but when I'm in the market for another car, I would love to get an '84.  I kinda' like the lines on the '84-'86 coupes the most on the notchback. I think it's because the nose is a little more pointy. <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000014||M King||12-07-1999||03:57 PM||||Thanks again for the great replys,<BR>I think I will go for a late model notchbach V6, maybe a 2m6 if I can find one. They seem to be the best priced and I like the notchback myself (not that fastback isn'n nice looking), I also have a few questions, did any models have a power antenna, and is the 4 cylinder a very powerfull engine in the fiero?? thanks again<BR> Mac||||reg||5
Z||000015||Monkeyman||12-07-1999||04:58 PM||||None of them came with power antennae, but there's a thread here (I think) explaining how someone put one on the rear.  I wouldn't call the 4 cyl "powerful" by any means, but that doesn't mean that you can't modify it.  Unless you have your heart set on an '84 Indy, find one with a 6.  It's got alot more potential.  It's also cheaper to get one from the start than to try to switch later.  Trust me on this one.  This is what I'm going through now.  And you WILL want one later.<p>[This message has been edited by Monkeyman (edited 12-07-1999).]||||reg||1
